{
  "id": "Compound::DB00281 - Gene::6331",
  "verbalization": "Lidocaine's relationship with Scn5a is supported by multiple pathways that converge through the intermediary of Prilocaine. The evidence suggests that Lidocaine, as an amide, is included in the broader category of anesthetics, which in turn includes Prilocaine. Prilocaine binds to Scn5a, a key subunit of the cardiac sodium channel, and this interaction is central to the mechanism of action of local anesthetics. The lowest common ancestors across these paths indicate a shared pathway involving monocarboxylic acid amides, reinforcing the structural and functional link between Lidocaine and Prilocaine, which ultimately interacts with Scn5a. The presence of a causal relationship between Lidocaine and Myocardial Depression, mediated by Prilocaine, further underscores the physiological impact of this interaction. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of anesthetics in modulating cardiac function through their interaction with ion channels, particularly Scn5a.",
